A||||11||Seanh||Tires||||1||
Z||000000||Seanh||10-14-2000||02:06 PM||||My car will be needing some new tires soon. I am wondering, I found out what the side wall markings mean. I was told the first number 205 is the width in MM at the tires widest point. The second, 60, is the aspect ratio. I am wondering, I now have 206/60's on the front and 215/60's on the back, will I notice better performance if I were to use 50 acpect ratio tires? and the same width tires? would I have speedo trouble with smaller tires like that? or should I just stick to 60's? What brands would you recommend? Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000001||Greg Piet||10-14-2000||02:53 PM||||My rule of thumb that I use when telling people what size tires should they buy...<BR>If you have to ask me, look on the door jamb and get those size tires for your car.<P>You can change all the aspect ratios you want, but YES just altering the profile number will make the speedo inaccurate (as the radius of the tire changes (thus your circumferance) your speedo becomes inacurate...   .<P>I think a much more important factor as far as performance is the tire compound...<BR>that opens a whole can of worms...<BR>How many miles must the tires last?  Do you drive in the rain with these tires, in the snow?<P>Heck you can get some kick butt tires that will school any of these 17" rim rubber band tires in your stock size.  Granted these tires will only last 10-15k miles, and they suck in the rain, but they ARE street legal.<P>I have had good luck with generic 300 treadwear all season tires in Goodyear, BFG...  BUT  nothing will compare to a DOT legal racing tire...<P>Check out  <A HREF="http://www.tirerack.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.tirerack.com</A> <BR><p>[This message has been edited by Greg Piet (edited 10-14-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Seanh||10-14-2000||05:02 PM||||The ones I am looking at are 420 treadware rating. They are fairly cheaply priced also. They have a traction rating of A. I usally drive in dry wether, but yes, I do drive it in the rain and ocasionally snow. I think I may just stick with the 60's and I still want to lower the car about 1.5" all around. How hard is it to recalibrate the speedo? Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||10-14-2000||06:05 PM||||The trick is to maintain the overall Diameter of the tire. I went from a 215/60R14 to a 225/50R15 and the difference was Only a 1.2% change in the speedo reading.<BR>To get an idea about how much tire sizes change your speedo go the the link below. You will need to reference the rear tire size since this is what the speedo is based upon. Just type in the rear size and the new size and it will give you all the data (including with and height in inches) the speedo error is near the bottom.<P> <A HREF="http://www.secret-secret.com/turbo/DIY/tirecalc/turbotire.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.secret-secret.com/turbo/DIY/tirecalc/turbotire.html</A> <p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 10-14-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Seanh||10-14-2000||06:50 PM||||Hey, Thanks. The is a great site. If I were to go with 50 or 55 tires, in order to make them pretty close on the speedo, could I go with somthing like 245's or 255's? Or would they be too wide? Also, how difficult is it to recal. the speedo, can I do it myself?<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||10-14-2000||07:32 PM||||I like that calculator. I hadn't seen that one before.||||reg||1
Z||000006||1FST2M6||10-14-2000||11:42 PM||||stick with the 205- 215 60s.. with a better tire than a 420 treadwear... if you wnat a good handeling tire don't get a weak tire with a lower aspect ratio and force it to be better.. you'll just be dissappointed.  <A HREF="http://www.discounttiredirect.com" TARGET=_blank>www.discounttiredirect.com</A>  or <A HREF="http://www.tirerack.com" TARGET=_blank>www.tirerack.com</A>  and check out the Yokohama AVS intermediate, AVS db, the BFG Comp, Eagle GS-N (Neon Cup tire). <P>------------------<BR>Shhh.. Top Secret! <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/1fstcheeto_2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Seanh||10-15-2000||12:45 PM||||I thought the higher the treadwear rating the longer the tire will last. Is that wrong, or are you saying not to get a high treadwear rating because of another reason? I was looking at the tires on carparts.com and the GOODYEAR EAGLE HP M+S: good wet handling and 380 tw rating. Or DUNLOP D60/A2 ALL WEATHER's: 320 tw rating. There are some others, but if you want to view the specs and tell me what you think. Go to <A HREF="http://www.Carparts.com" TARGET=_blank>www.Carparts.com</A>  , select Pontiac and 1986 and then Fiero GT 2.8L. On the next page select tires and choose 215(rear) 205(front) and 60/15", also check High Performance and Ultra-High Performance and then Search. I am considering any of these tires but I am unsure what ones I should get. Speed rating isnt really a biggie because I never go at those speeds unless racing and there not sustained for long at all. What do you guys think about the ones on that page? Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000008||1FST2M6||10-15-2000||12:51 PM||||your right the higher the treadwear the longer it will last.. the lower the treadwear the stickier the tire!.. thus you want a 140-280 treadwear.. unless you get a Nitto NT555 the CLAIM a tread wear of 300 and they are GREAT tires! the Yokohamas i had were A509 (tw 160) A032 (tw 80) and A520 (tw 180) and ALL were VERY hard pressed to break loose!  GT Bastard has the A509s and seems happy with them... (we're mine) <P>------------------<BR>Shhh.. Top Secret! <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/1fstcheeto_2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Will||10-15-2000||01:51 PM||||Recalibating the speedometer requires some electronics knowledge.<BR>     If the right gears were still available, it would be a three minute job, but all the odd sized gears have been discontinued.<BR>     I am running 245/60-15 Dunlop GT Qualifiers on the rear (they had the virtue of fitting my budget).<BR>     I built an oscillator using a Schmidt trigger inverter with an RC feedback loop. I intercept the signal from the stock reference oscillator crystal and instead use my oscillator as a reference. My oscillator, of course, is adjustable. I can alter the speedometer calibration by +/- 10-12%, depending on how big a pot I use.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Formula 18||10-15-2000||09:54 PM||||If you went with a smaller tire on a 14 in rim wouln't you get a better Gear ratio and less side wall roll? ||||reg||1
Z||000011||bHooper||10-16-2000||03:18 PM||||i am putting Dunlop Sp Sport 5000, 225/60ZR- 15s on the rear of my GT, tommorow.  i had wanted to hold off until i got the suspension finished, but one nail and one bolt had other ideas.<P>as for price; discount tire/tire rack, wanted $68 ea. + s/h+$12 ea having them installed.  i am getting them locally for $89, mounted and ballanced.  a fair trade off considering i only have to wait one day, not 2 or 3.<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
